Q: Casting in comparator functions for qsort A: <p>There are two imporant remarks according to the comparison function for the standard C function <strong>qsort</strong>. The first one is  (the C24 Standard, section &quot;7.24.5 Searching and sorting utilities&quot;):</p>
<p>2 <strong>The implementation shall ensure that</strong> the second argument of the comparison function (when called from bsearch), or <strong>both arguments (when called from qsort), are pointers to elements of the array</strong>. The first argument when called from bsearch shall equal key.</p>
<p>And the second one is</p>
<p>3 <strong>The comparison function shall not alter the contents of the array</strong>. <strong>The implementation may reorder elements of the array between calls to the comparison function, but shall not alter the contents of any individual element.</strong></p>
<p>The comparison function itself is declared the following way</p>
<pre><code>int compar(const void *a, const void *b);
</code></pre>
<p>So if elements of an array has the type <strong>T *^n</strong> then within the comparison funtion you need to cast the direferenced pointer to <strong>const void</strong> <strong>a</strong> or <strong>b</strong> like</p>
<pre><code>T *^n const element = *( T *^n const * )a;
</code></pre>
<p>Or you may even write</p>
<pre><code>T *^n const element = *( T *^n const ( * ) )a;
</code></pre>
<p>That is the pointer to the incomplete constant type <strong>const</strong> <strong>void</strong> must be casted to a pointer to the constant element type <strong>const <em>array_element_type</em></strong>.  It means that you must qualify the passed by pointers elements of an array as constant elements. If the array element type has a pointer type then within the comparison function it shall be considered as a constant pointer type.</p>
<p>Let's consider the following demonstration program.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

int string_cmp( const void *a, const void *b )
{
    const char * const s1 = *( const char * const * )a;
    const char * const s2 = *( const char * const * )b;

    return strcmp( s1, s2 );
}

int main(void) 
{
    const char *s[] = { &quot;World&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot; };
    const size_t N = sizeof( s ) / sizeof( *s );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, s[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

    qsort( s, N, sizeof( *s ), string_cmp );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, s[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

}
</code></pre>
<p>The program output is</p>
<pre><code>World Hello 
Hello World 
</code></pre>
<p>In the above program elements of the array are pointers. So within the comparison function they are processed as constant pointers. You can rewrite the program the following way.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

typedef const char * T;

int string_cmp( const void *a, const void *b )
{
    const T s1 = *( T const * )a;
    const T s2 = *( T const * )b;

    return strcmp( s1, s2 );
}

int main(void) 
{
    T s[] = { &quot;World&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot; };
    const size_t N = sizeof( s ) / sizeof( *s );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, s[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

    qsort( s, N, sizeof( *s ), string_cmp );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, s[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

}
</code></pre>
<p>If you have for example an array of an arithmetic type then you can write</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

int cmp( const void *a, const void *b )
{
    int x = *( int const * )a;
    int y = *( int const * )b;

    return ( y &lt; x ) - ( x &lt; y );
}

int main(void) 
{
    int a[] = { 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 };
    const size_t N = sizeof( a ) / sizeof( *a );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%d &quot;, a[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

    qsort( a, N, sizeof( *a ), cmp );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%d &quot;, a[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

}
</code></pre>
<p>The program output is</p>
<pre><code>5 4 3 2 1 
1 2 3 4 5
</code></pre>
<p>Within the comparison function the record of the casting type as for example in this line</p>
<pre><code>int x = *( int const * )a;
</code></pre>
<p>is equivalent to</p>
<pre><code>int x = *( const int * )a;
</code></pre>
<p>As for your question</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What is then the correct policy for accessing the array elements, is a single dereference sufficient (e.g. <code>*pa</code> or <code>pa-&gt;foo</code>) or should it be multiple dereferences, based on <code>*^n</code>?l</p>
</blockquote>
<p>then all depends on the method of sorting an array of compound elements when it can be for example an array of pointers to structures that in turn contain pointers as its data members and so on. Moreover the criteria of sorting can be based on several different data members of elements of the array.</p>
<p>Consider the following simple example. You have an array of pointers to structures that in turn contain as their data members pointers to string literals. And you want to sort the array of structures by the first character of pointed string literals. A corresponding program can look the following way..</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

struct S
{
    const char *s;  
};

int struct_cmp( const void *a, const void *b )
{
    struct S *s1 = *( struct S * const * )a;
    struct S *s2 = *( struct S * const * )b;

    return ( *s2-&gt;s &lt; *s1-&gt;s ) - ( *s1-&gt;s &lt; *s2-&gt;s );
}

int main(void) 
{
    struct S s1 = { .s = &quot;World&quot; };
    struct S s2 = { .s = &quot;Hello&quot; };

    struct S * a[] = { &amp;s1, &amp;s2 };

    const size_t N = sizeof( a ) / sizeof( *a );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, a[i]-&gt;s );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

    qsort( a, N, sizeof( *a ), struct_cmp );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, a[i]-&gt;s );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );
}
</code></pre>
<p>The program output is</p>
<pre><code>World Hello 
Hello World 
</code></pre>
<p>Pay attention to  that within the comparison functions there is no necessary to write</p>
<pre><code>const char * const s1 = *( const char * const * )a;
const char * const s2 = *( const char * const * )b;
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre><code>struct S * const s1 = *( struct S * const * )a;
struct S * const s2 = *( struct S * const * )b;
</code></pre>
<p>instead of</p>
<pre><code>const char *s1 = *( const char * const * )a;
const char *s2 = *( const char * const * )b;
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre><code>struct S *s1 = *( struct S * const * )a;
struct S *s2 = *( struct S * const * )b;
</code></pre>
<p>because a value of a constant object may be assigned to a non-constant object.</p>

Q: Command line get path to gem A: <p>Put a bash function in your <code>.bashrc</code> (or wherever) called <code>gemdir</code> which looks like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>gemdir() {
    ruby -e &quot;begin; puts Gem::Specification::find_by_name('$1').gem_dir; rescue Gem::MissingSpecError; end&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$ gemdir jekyll
=&gt; /home/me/.rbenv/versions/4.0.1/lib/ruby/gems/4.0.0/gems/jekyll-4.4.1
$ gemdir jekyl
=&gt; # nothing
</code></pre>

Q: The date field is not entered correctly using Selenium Python A: <p>The main challenge here seems to be how to input the date.</p>
<p>It is possible to navigate the date-picker by using left- and right-arrow buttons until such time as the date-picker title shows the appropriate month and year. You can then use an XPATH selector to locate and click the relevant day.</p>
<p>If the date that you actually want to specify differs wildly from the current date this is going to be very inefficient.</p>
<p>Think of this merely as a demo' and not a recommendation.</p>
<pre><code>import calendar
from selenium.webdriver.support.ui import WebDriverWait
from selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions as EC
from selenium.webdriver.common.by import By
from selenium.webdriver import Chrome

URL = &quot;https://bo.helios12.com/&quot;
USERNAME = &quot;test2@washbucklers.com&quot;
PASSWORD = &quot;welcome1&quot;
MONTHS = list(calendar.month_name)
# for DD and MM ensure 2 digits and YYYY 4 digits
DD = &quot;10&quot;
MM = &quot;12&quot;
YYYY = &quot;2025&quot;
NEXT = &quot;#calRptDate_nextArrow&quot;
PREV = &quot;#calRptDate_prevArrow&quot;

# pylint: disable=possibly-used-before-assignment

def send(css, text, *, clear=True):
    ec = EC.element_to_be_clickable
    loc = (By.CSS_SELECTOR, css)
    field = WAIT.until(ec(loc))
    if clear:
        field.clear()
    field.send_keys(text)

def click(css):
    loc = (By.CSS_SELECTOR, css)
    ec = EC.element_to_be_clickable
    WAIT.until(ec(loc)).click()

def switch():
    ec = EC.frame_to_be_available_and_switch_to_it
    loc = (By.TAG_NAME, &quot;iframe&quot;)
    WAIT.until(ec(loc))

def get_title_text():
    ec = EC.presence_of_element_located
    loc = (By.CSS_SELECTOR, &quot;#calRptDate_title&quot;)
    return WAIT.until(ec(loc)).text

def get_year():
    _, yyyy = get_title_text().split()
    return yyyy

def get_month():
    mm, _ = get_title_text().split(&quot;,&quot;)
    return f&quot;{MONTHS.index(mm):02d}&quot;

def move_to_year():
    while (yyyy := get_year()) != YYYY:
        click(PREV if yyyy &gt; YYYY else NEXT)

def move_to_month():
    while (mm := get_month()) != MM:
        click(PREV if mm &gt; MM else NEXT)

def click_day():
    ec = EC.element_to_be_clickable
    loc = (By.XPATH, f&quot;//table[@id='calRptDate_daysTable']//tbody//td/div[text()='{DD}']&quot;)
    WAIT.until(ec(loc)).click()

if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    with Chrome() as DRIVER:
        DRIVER.get(URL)
        WAIT = WebDriverWait(DRIVER, 10)
        send(&quot;input[type='email']&quot;, USERNAME)
        send(&quot;input[type='password']&quot;, PASSWORD)
        click(&quot;button[type='submit']&quot;)
        click(&quot;a[href='/Reports']&quot;)
        click(&quot;a[href='/Reports/Sales']&quot;)
        click(&quot;a[href='/Reports/Sales/ShiftReport']&quot;)
        switch()
        click(&quot;#txtRptDate&quot;) # activates the date picker
        move_to_year()
        move_to_month()
        click_day()
        input(&quot;Press return: &quot;)

</code></pre>
